    "case_id": "766",
    "case_summary": "A 42-year-old Caucasian man with a 12-year history of well-controlled HIV (undetectable viral load, CD4+ count 1184 × 10^6 cell/liter). Past medical history includes treatment for early latent syphilis six years prior. The patient presented with low-grade fever (37.5°) and sudden appearance of skin lesions at the base of his penis. He reported recent sexual activity with multiple male partners. Two days after the initial skin manifestations, he developed fever up to 39°C, chest pain without irradiation, and shortness of breath.\n\nInitial examination revealed vesicular and umbilicated lesions on genitals and small lymphadenomegaly in the groin area. No oropharyngeal lesions were detected. Upon admission for chest symptoms, the patient was hemodynamically stable with continuous anterior chest pain exacerbated by deep breathing.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * High-sensitivity cardiac troponin: 128.00 pg/ml initially, peaked at 142.00 pg/ml (reference value < 14 pg/ml)    * Multiple viral serologies including CMV, EBV, Coxsackievirus, Echovirus, Parvovirus B19, and Herpes virus 1/2 were negative (except for past CMV and EBV exposure)    - Imaging Studies:    * ECG: Showed sinus tachycardia with elevated PR segment in aVR lead and depressed in peripheral and thoracic leads    * Echocardiography: Left ventricle diastolic diameter of 59 mm, hypokinetic left ventricle with EF 50%    * Chest CT angiography: Normal, no pulmonary embolism or pneumonia    * Cardiac MRI: Showed slightly increased global T1 and T2 mapping values, minimal hyperintensity of pericardium without effusion, normal biventricular function (EF 57%)    * Coronary CT angiography: Normal    - Continuous Monitoring:    * ECG monitoring showed persistent episodes of sinus pauses (longest 10.1 seconds)    * Episodes of advanced AV block grade 2 Mobitz type 1 and 2    * Sinus bradycardia (heart rate 40-45, nadir 32 pulses/minute while sleeping)",
